/T (Subspace methods: PCA describes images using a few orthogonal directions that capture most of the variance [3]. White noise spreads its own energy equally in all directions, while the content of the image piles up in the first few, so keeping only the leading components improves the signal-to- noise ratio. The real challenge is how many to retain: too few leads to loss in detail, too many and the noise comes back.)
/E (Subspace methods: PCA describes images using a few orthogonal directions that capture most of the variance [3]. White noise spreads its own energy equally in all directions, while the content of the image piles up in the first few, so keeping only the leading components improves the signal-to- noise ratio. The real challenge is how many to retain: too few leads to loss in detail, too many and the noise comes back.)
