/T (The Gaussian, median and bilateral filters come from OpenCV. For each noise condition we chose the parameters by grid search on the 200 validation images, maximising mean PSNR. The grids were: Gaussian kernel size {3, 5} and spread {0.5, 0.8, 1.0, 1.5}; median kernel size {3, 5}; bilateral diameter {5, 7, 9}, range spread {0.05, 0.10, 0.15, 0.25} and spatial spread {1.5, 3.0}. The selected values are in Table II. This does give the filters an advantage, since they are tuned knowing the noise type and level, which the learned models are never told.)
/E (The Gaussian, median and bilateral filters come from OpenCV. For each noise condition we chose the parameters by grid search on the 200 validation images, maximising mean PSNR. The grids were: Gaussian kernel size {3, 5} and spread {0.5, 0.8, 1.0, 1.5}; median kernel size {3, 5}; bilateral diameter {5, 7, 9}, range spread {0.05, 0.10, 0.15, 0.25} and spatial spread {1.5, 3.0}. The selected values are in Table II. This does give the filters an advantage, since they are tuned knowing the noise type and level, which the learned models are never told.)
