/Title (Subspace methods: PCA describes images using a few orthogonal directions that capture most of the variance [3]. White noise spreads its own energy equally in all directions, while the content of the image piles up in the first few, so keeping only the leading components improves the signal-to- noise ratio. The real challenge is how many to retain: too few leads to loss in detail, too many and the noise comes back.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 427 0 R
/Prev 431 0 R
/Next 451 0 R
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Title (Most of this work targets large natural photographs and compares against a handful of rivals. Small, centred objects, impulse noise next to Gaussian noise, and the question of what happens when the input is only lightly corrupted get much less attention. This paper looks at exactly those cases, using a deliberately small experiment that anyone can rerun.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 427 0 R
/Prev 452 0 R
/Next 432 0 R
>>
endobj
